---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../config";

const aboutUrl = new URL("/about/", SITE.url).href;
const authorUrl = new URL(SITE.author.url, SITE.url).href;

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  url: aboutUrl,
  mainEntity: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: SITE.name,
    url: SITE.url,
    description: SITE.description,
    founder: { "@type": "Person", name: SITE.author.name, url: authorUrl },
  },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`${SITE.name} is an independent site of crochet tutorials, charts, and beginner-friendly patterns, published by ${SITE.author.name}.`}
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-10 sm:px-8 sm:py-14">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
      About {SITE.name}
    </h1>

    <div class="prose mt-8">
      <p>
        {SITE.name} teaches crochet to beginners in plain US English: what to
        buy, how to read a pattern, the basic stitches, shaping, color changes,
        finishing, and gauge, followed by small projects that use those skills.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm <a href={SITE.author.url}>{SITE.author.name}</a>, and I publish
        and edit the site. I am not a crochet designer or a certified
        instructor. My job here is to make sure the instructions are clear, the
        numbers add up, and the pictures match the words.
      </p>

      <h2>What the site teaches</h2>
      <p>
        The spine is the <a href="/start-here/">12-step course</a>. Beside it
        sit reference <a href="/charts/">charts</a> for when you need a number,
        <a href="/tutorials/">tutorials</a> that teach one technique each, and
        <a href="/free-patterns/">free patterns</a> for small projects. Every
        page uses US crochet terms. Where a UK term names a different stitch,
        the page says so.
      </p>

      <h2>How instructions are checked</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Stitch arithmetic.</strong> Every round and row total is
          checked on paper: the increases in a round add up to the number in
          parentheses, and a panel's size follows from its stated gauge.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Charts.</strong> Hook and yarn tables are written from
          published standards, such as the Craft Yarn Council yarn weight system
          and manufacturer hook sizing, not copied from other blogs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Diagrams.</strong> Step diagrams are drawn for the page they
          sit on and are checked against its written counts.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What has and has not been tested</h2>
      <p>
        Checking a pattern on paper is not the same as making it. The sizes on
        this site are worked out from gauge rather than measured from a finished
        sample, so a finished size is a calculation and yardage is an estimate.
        Each pattern page says so near the top. Pictures of finished projects
        are illustrations of the intended look, labeled as such, and never
        photographs of a made sample. How those illustrations are produced is
        explained on the <a href="/disclosures/">disclosures page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Publishing history</h2>
      <p>
        The site went live on September 10, 2026 as The Crochet Guide with the
        first technique articles and charts. Halloween and Christmas tutorials
        followed in mid-September. It was renamed {SITE.name} on September 30,
        2026, and most of the project patterns were published in early October
        2026. Every article shows the day it was first published and, if it
        has changed since, the day it was last materially updated.
      </p>

      <h2>Report an error</h2>
      <p>
        If a stitch count, a measurement, or a step looks wrong, tell me through
        the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> and name the page and the row.
        Correct reports get the page fixed, and the article's updated date
        changes with it.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site will not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Republish another designer's pattern. Patterns are their authors'
          work. When a pattern is featured here, the link goes to the designer's
          own page so they get the visit.
        </li>
        <li>
          Present an illustration as a photograph of something that was made.
        </li>
        <li>
          Recommend a product for a commission. The
          <a href="/disclosures/">disclosures page</a> explains how the gear
          guides work.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How the site pays for itself</h2>
      <p>
        Display advertising and affiliate links in the hooks and yarn guides.
        Both are explained on the <a href="/disclosures/">disclosures page</a>,
        and neither costs you anything. Every tutorial and chart is free.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
